<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-line-plane-hyperplane-eqns">
<title>Determining equations of lines/planes/hyperplanes</title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	determine the equation of the line/plane/hyperplane that passes through point <m> P </m> and satisfies the stated geometric condition.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(2,3) </m>;
		line normal to the vector <m> \uvec{v} = (1,-3) </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
		the line is
		<md>
			\dotprod{(1,-3)}{\bbrac{(x,y) - (2,3)}} = 0
			\quad \implies \quad
			x - 3 y = -7
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(3,0) </m>;
		line parallel to the line <m> 5 x + 6 y = 3 </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <m> \R^2 </m>,
		parallel lines have parallel normal vectors,
		so we may use <m> \uvec{n} = (5,6) </m> as a normal vector for the desired line.
		Using <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
		the line is
		<md>
			\dotprod{(5,6)}{\bbrac{(x,y) - (3,0)}} = 0
			\quad \implies \quad
			5 x + 6 y = 15
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(-4,-3,6) </m>;
		plane normal to the vector <m> \uvec{v} = (1,-1,6) </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
		the plane is
		<md>
			\dotprod{(1,-1,6)}{\bbrac{(x,y,z) - (-4,-3,6)}} = 0
			\quad \implies \quad
			x - y + 6 z = 35
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(3,-6,1) </m>;
		plane parallel to the plane <m> 2 x - 5 y + 5 z = 4 </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <m> \R^3 </m>,
		parallel planes have parallel normal vectors,
		so we may use <m> \uvec{n} = (2,-5,5) </m> as a normal vector for the desired plane.
		Using <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
		the plane is
		<md>
			\dotprod{(2,-5,5)}{\bbrac{(x,y) - (3,-6,1)}} = 0
			\quad \implies \quad
			2 x - 5 y + 5z = 41
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m> P(3,-5,6,5) </m>;
		hyperplane normal to the vector <m> \uvec{v} = (4,3,-3,-3) </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Using <term>point-normal form</term>,
		the hyperplane is
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> \dotprod{(4,3,-3,-3)}{\bbrac{(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4) - (3,-5,6,5)}} = 0 </mrow>
			<mrow> \implies \quad 4 x_1 + 3 x_2 - 3 x_3 - 3 x_4 = -36 </mrow>
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p><m> P(5,5,-4,-3); </m></p>

		<p> hyperplane parallel to the hyperplane <m> 3 x_1 - 2 x_2 - 4 x_3 - 2 x_4 = -2 </m>. </p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		In <m> \R^4 </m>,
		parallel hyperplanes have parallel normal vectors,
		so we may use <m> \uvec{n} = (3,-2,-4,-2) </m> as a normal vector for the desired hyperplane.
		Using <term>point-normal form</term>,
		the hyperplane is
		<md alignment="gather">
			<mrow> \dotprod{(3,-2,-4,-2)}{\bbrac{(x,y) - (5,5,-4,-3)}} = 0 </mrow>
			<mrow> \implies \quad 3 x_1 - 2 x_2 - 4 x_3 - 2 x_4 = 27 </mrow>
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
